1
00:00:17,784 --> 00:00:22,255
[Bret] <i>What do you do
when your friends think
you’re the boy who cried wolf?</i>

2
00:00:28,562 --> 00:00:33,566
<i>Matt Kellner had gone missing
in the dead of night sometime
in September of 1981.</i>

3
00:00:33,567 --> 00:00:35,602
[breathing shakily]

4
00:00:36,670 --> 00:00:41,107
[Bret] <i>But oddly, no one
in my social circle
was that concerned about it.</i>

5
00:00:42,776 --> 00:00:46,580
<i>Looking back, I realize that’s
the gift of youth.</i>

6
00:00:47,548 --> 00:00:49,649
<i>You think you’ll live forever.</i>

7
00:00:49,650 --> 00:00:52,786
I bet Matt moved to Big Sur
to deal drugs,
get out of the LA rat race.

8
00:00:52,787 --> 00:00:54,653
[Robert] I agree.

9
00:00:54,654 --> 00:00:56,388
I mean, who wouldn’t?
I hear
the Esalen Institute’s amazing.

10
00:00:56,389 --> 00:00:58,125
What?

11
00:00:58,325 --> 00:01:00,194
That’s lunacy. What about
the blood-stained backpack
they found?

12
00:01:00,393 --> 00:01:04,965
[sighs] Hon, I love you,
but why are you so drawn
to worst-case scenarios?

13
00:01:05,131 --> 00:01:06,766
You didn’t even know him.

14
00:01:06,767 --> 00:01:09,302
Don’t mourn a death that
hasn’t officially happened.

15
00:01:09,569 --> 00:01:12,739
- Can we talk
about happier things?
- Please. I need a cig.

16
00:01:12,740 --> 00:01:13,974
[Susan chuckles]

17
00:01:15,275 --> 00:01:20,347
[Bret] <i>But then came
October 1st, the so-called
first day of spooky season.</i>

18
00:01:23,650 --> 00:01:28,388
<i>October 1st was officially
The Day the Fun Died.</i>

19
00:01:31,324 --> 00:01:34,861
<i>Because that’s the day
The Trawler got Really Rhonda.</i>

20
00:01:34,862 --> 00:01:36,763
[♪ dark synth music plays]

21
00:01:45,705 --> 00:01:47,140
[all laughing]

22
00:01:47,807 --> 00:01:51,245
[Bret] <i>Really Rhonda was in
our class,
and everyone loved her.</i>

23
00:01:51,778 --> 00:01:53,980
<i>Everyone thought
she was hilarious.</i>

24
00:01:53,981 --> 00:01:57,783
<i>Her father was a big ICM agent
who represented
Sally Kellerman.</i>

25
00:01:57,784 --> 00:01:59,719
<i>He paid the school
a handsome fee every year</i>

26
00:01:59,720 --> 00:02:01,922
<i>to assure that Rhonda
would be assimilated</i>

27
00:02:02,088 --> 00:02:06,092
<i>and not locked away
in the dungeon
that was a special needs class.</i>

28
00:02:09,429 --> 00:02:13,466
If X and B are both
positive real numbers...

29
00:02:13,567 --> 00:02:15,335
[Bret] <i>Today
we would understand</i>

30
00:02:15,336 --> 00:02:17,938
<i>that Rhonda suffered
from a neurological condition,</i>

31
00:02:18,104 --> 00:02:21,541
<i>but back then we just thought
she was darkly
and convulsively...</i>

32
00:02:24,544 --> 00:02:26,180
- [giggles]
<i>- ...impulsive.</i>

33
00:02:26,313 --> 00:02:28,315
- [students laugh]
- Rhonda, please stop it.

34
00:02:28,682 --> 00:02:30,817
I wanna fuck Chewbacca, motherfucker.

35
00:02:30,818 --> 00:02:32,752
- [Bret] <i>And a real
truth teller.</i>
- [students laugh]

36
00:02:33,019 --> 00:02:36,389
- [Flanigan] Rhonda,
do you want detention?
- Um...

37
00:02:39,226 --> 00:02:41,394
George Washington sucks cocks
in hell, motherfucker!

38
00:02:41,395 --> 00:02:43,663
Suck it, lick it,
fucking ass-face! Fuck.

39
00:02:43,664 --> 00:02:47,568
- [students laugh]
- Really, Rhonda? Really?

40
00:02:49,035 --> 00:02:52,339
[Bret] <i>And from that moment on,
that’s what we all called her.</i>

41
00:02:53,907 --> 00:02:55,408
<i>Really Rhonda.</i>

42
00:02:59,346 --> 00:03:01,648
[♪ ambient music playing]

43
00:03:05,585 --> 00:03:08,388
<i>I always felt an affection
for Rhonda.</i>

44
00:03:10,023 --> 00:03:11,724
<i>Because during one
of her outbursts,</i>

45
00:03:11,725 --> 00:03:14,160
<i>she could have easily
told the truth
about me,</i>

46
00:03:14,161 --> 00:03:16,730
<i>called me a fag or a gayface,</i>

47
00:03:17,297 --> 00:03:18,598
<i>but she was kind to me.</i>

48
00:03:19,332 --> 00:03:22,736
<i>I felt she purposely edited out
my secret truth.</i>

49
00:03:24,337 --> 00:03:29,309
<i>Protected me somehow from
the pubescent cruelty
of the other kids.</i>

50
00:03:39,386 --> 00:03:41,487
<i>My favorite thing about Rhonda</i>

51
00:03:41,488 --> 00:03:46,426
<i>was that she would not
get off the disco roller trend
long after it was over.</i>

52
00:03:46,893 --> 00:03:49,797
<i>She just didn’t care
what people thought about her.</i>

53
00:03:50,698 --> 00:03:53,133
<i>Can you imagine
the gift of that?</i>

54
00:03:53,333 --> 00:03:57,437
Really Rhonda is such
a good skater.
She’s even better than Cher.

55
00:04:29,202 --> 00:04:32,005
[Bret] <i>Disco roller skating
is important to this tale,</i>

56
00:04:32,439 --> 00:04:35,142
<i>because that’s
what she was doing
when she was taken.</i>

57
00:04:35,242 --> 00:04:37,411
[♪ "Don’t Stand
So Close to Me" plays]

58
00:04:38,545 --> 00:04:43,684
<i>There was only one disco roller
derby place left
in town in 1981.</i>

59
00:04:44,218 --> 00:04:48,022
<i>And Rhonda was the only patron
at Wheels that night
of October 1st.</i>

60
00:04:48,756 --> 00:04:52,025
<i>The Trawler knew that,
the loneliness of the place.</i>

61
00:04:53,426 --> 00:04:57,864
<i>And on that night, he snatched
Really Rhonda
from her happy place.</i>

62
00:04:58,031 --> 00:05:00,033
♪<i>♪ Young teacher ♪</i>

63
00:05:00,200 --> 00:05:01,767
<i>And out of all of our lives.</i>

64
00:05:01,768 --> 00:05:03,404
♪<i>♪ The subject of
Schoolgirl fantasy ♪</i>

65
00:05:03,537 --> 00:05:07,273
♪<i>♪ She wants him so badly ♪</i>

66
00:05:07,274 --> 00:05:10,277
♪<i>♪ Knows what she wants to be ♪</i>

67
00:05:11,011 --> 00:05:14,313
♪<i>♪ Inside her, there’s longing ♪</i>

68
00:05:14,314 --> 00:05:17,483
♪<i>♪ This girl’s an open page ♪</i>

69
00:05:17,484 --> 00:05:20,987
♪<i>♪ Bookmarking,
She’s so close now ♪</i>

70
00:05:21,121 --> 00:05:23,891
♪<i>♪ This girl is half his age ♪</i>

71
00:05:24,558 --> 00:05:27,794
♪<i>♪ Don’t stand, don’t stand so ♪</i>

72
00:05:27,795 --> 00:05:30,797
♪<i>♪ Don’t stand so close to me ♪</i>

73
00:05:31,431 --> 00:05:34,534
♪<i>♪ Don’t stand, don’t stand so ♪</i>

74
00:05:34,535 --> 00:05:38,238
♪<i>♪ Don’t stand so close to me ♪</i>

75
00:05:39,973 --> 00:05:43,009
♪<i>♪ Her friends are so jealous ♪</i>

76
00:05:43,143 --> 00:05:46,046
♪<i>♪ You know how bad girls get ♪</i>

77
00:05:46,613 --> 00:05:50,182
♪<i>♪ Sometimes it’s not so easy ♪</i>

78
00:05:50,183 --> 00:05:53,219
♪<i>♪ To be the teacher’s pet ♪</i>

79
00:05:53,220 --> 00:05:54,720
♪<i>♪ Temptation... ♪</i>

80
00:05:54,721 --> 00:05:56,523
[Bret] <i>When Rhonda
missed her curfew...</i>

81
00:05:56,656 --> 00:05:59,726
♪<i>♪ So bad it makes him cry ♪</i>

82
00:06:00,026 --> 00:06:02,263
<i>...her parents reported
her missing.</i>

83
00:06:02,563 --> 00:06:03,663
[siren blares]

84
00:06:05,665 --> 00:06:08,602
[police chattering]

85
00:06:08,969 --> 00:06:12,238
[Bret] <i>The police immediately
checked out
the scene at Wheels.</i>

86
00:06:12,239 --> 00:06:14,040
- I’ll check around back.
- Got it.

87
00:06:17,010 --> 00:06:19,045
[Bret] <i>And that’s when
they found bloodstains.</i>

88
00:06:19,246 --> 00:06:20,613
[officer] Over here.

89
00:06:20,614 --> 00:06:22,649
[Bret] <i>Telltale clues
of a violent struggle.</i>

90
00:06:24,317 --> 00:06:26,953
<i>I went to the scene
of the crime the next day,</i>

91
00:06:26,954 --> 00:06:29,156
<i>oddly, instinctively.</i>

92
00:06:29,689 --> 00:06:33,693
<i>The place Rhonda
had joyously twirled
the night before was deserted.</i>

93
00:06:34,027 --> 00:06:40,066
<i>The rotating disco ball,
once a symbol of joy,
seemed sad to me, mournful.</i>

94
00:06:40,534 --> 00:06:44,037
<i>Slowly twirling like
a flag at half-mast</i>

95
00:06:44,204 --> 00:06:49,042
<i>to announce
all the ends of things
that 1981 had taken from us.</i>

96
00:06:50,510 --> 00:06:51,612
<i>Matt.</i>

97
00:06:52,112 --> 00:06:53,380
<i>Roller disco.</i>

98
00:06:54,214 --> 00:06:55,482
<i>Really Rhonda.</i>

99
00:06:57,417 --> 00:06:59,319
<i>And our sense of security.</i>

100
00:07:03,958 --> 00:07:05,759
[♪ sinister music plays]

101
00:07:53,407 --> 00:07:55,675
[Rhonda whimpering]
Please, please.

102
00:07:55,676 --> 00:07:57,643
Please, please, help me.

103
00:07:57,644 --> 00:07:59,112
[whimpers]

104
00:08:02,449 --> 00:08:04,251
[breathing shakily]

105
00:08:07,287 --> 00:08:08,421
[gasps]

106
00:08:16,129 --> 00:08:17,464
[whimpering]

107
00:08:22,402 --> 00:08:23,904
[The Trawler] Eat it.

108
00:08:24,137 --> 00:08:25,572
[screaming]

109
00:08:39,252 --> 00:08:42,422
[Bret] <i>It took about three days
for everything
to go back to normal,</i>

110
00:08:43,056 --> 00:08:45,325
<i>and for everyone
to forget about Matt</i>

111
00:08:46,426 --> 00:08:47,328
<i>and Rhonda.</i>

112
00:08:47,527 --> 00:08:50,363
[no audible dialogue]

113
00:09:01,208 --> 00:09:03,075
[Debbie] Oh, my God.

114
00:09:03,076 --> 00:09:05,344
I love it when we skip lunch
on the quad
and kick it at the Hamlet.

115
00:09:05,345 --> 00:09:06,346
[Debbie] Yeah, me too.

116
00:09:06,546 --> 00:09:08,615
Anyone want half my lunch?

117
00:09:09,450 --> 00:09:11,218
- [Robert] Ooh.
- [chuckles] Oh.

118
00:09:13,119 --> 00:09:15,589
Okay. [giggles]

119
00:09:17,123 --> 00:09:18,225
Okay, listen up.

120
00:09:18,826 --> 00:09:20,760
Don’t forget
to take your Flintstones
vitamins this month--

121
00:09:20,761 --> 00:09:22,528
Or your diet pills.

122
00:09:22,529 --> 00:09:25,165
Saturday is Robert’s,
and next Thursday is <i>Dance Off!</i>

123
00:09:25,298 --> 00:09:28,302
and no one is allowed
to be hungover

124
00:09:28,435 --> 00:09:31,538
because on Sunday,
we all need to practice.

125
00:09:33,473 --> 00:09:34,574
Seriously?

126
00:09:35,275 --> 00:09:37,544
That <i>Dance Off!</i>
thing is really happening?

127
00:09:37,677 --> 00:09:39,880
Oh, my God.
I thought you were joking.

128
00:09:40,780 --> 00:09:42,849
Does it look
like I’m joking, Bret?

129
00:09:42,850 --> 00:09:45,585
- [♪ "Rapture" plays]
- [dancers cheering]

130
00:09:50,657 --> 00:09:53,527
[Bret] Dance Off! <i>was
a daily televised
cable access show</i>

131
00:09:53,693 --> 00:09:57,731
<i>that featured regular
teenagers dancing
to the hot hits of the day.</i>

132
00:09:59,699 --> 00:10:01,434
<i>Teenagers like us.</i>

133
00:10:05,372 --> 00:10:07,473
<i>A Los Angeles institution,</i>

134
00:10:07,474 --> 00:10:11,745
<i>it had its heyday in 1974
and refused to die.</i>

135
00:10:14,281 --> 00:10:18,652
♪<i>♪ Toe to toe
Dancing very close ♪</i>

136
00:10:18,752 --> 00:10:21,288
[Bret] <i>Debbie had been obsessed
with the idea</i>

137
00:10:21,388 --> 00:10:24,590
<i>ever since Corazon Cha Cha
Rodriguez had gone on
and held the camera’s attention</i>

138
00:10:24,591 --> 00:10:27,227
<i>for the length
of an entire Blondie song.</i>

139
00:10:28,062 --> 00:10:29,763
<i>And not just any Blondie song.</i>

140
00:10:30,063 --> 00:10:34,134
<i>"Rapture," six minutes
and 31 seconds.</i>

141
00:10:35,169 --> 00:10:39,105
<i>Debbie saw that hot lava fame
and she wanted it for herself.</i>

142
00:10:39,106 --> 00:10:43,342
<i>Becoming a star was
her secret ambition, but she
was too cool to ever admit it.</i>

143
00:10:43,343 --> 00:10:45,545
♪<i>♪ Stepping lightly ♪</i>

144
00:10:45,546 --> 00:10:50,950
♪<i>♪ Hang each night in rapture ♪</i>

145
00:10:50,951 --> 00:10:53,120
I gotta say, I hate that show.

146
00:10:53,253 --> 00:10:57,090
Okay, well, it’s a rite
of passage classic in
this town, and it’s happening.

147
00:10:57,290 --> 00:11:01,827
Guys, don’t you
think it’s kind of
a bad look to be partying on TV

148
00:11:01,828 --> 00:11:04,231
- with classmates missing?
- [sighing] Okay.

149
00:11:04,431 --> 00:11:08,067
Well, it’s what I want. It’s
done, and you’re all coming.

150
00:11:08,068 --> 00:11:11,304
You’re welcome. [sighs]

151
00:11:12,405 --> 00:11:14,306
Okay, so that’s it then?

152
00:11:14,307 --> 00:11:17,878
We just don’t care anymore
about Rhonda or Matt?

153
00:11:18,144 --> 00:11:20,981
Look, Bret, I’m not trying
to speak for the group here,

154
00:11:21,114 --> 00:11:22,915
but I’m gonna speak
for the group.

155
00:11:22,916 --> 00:11:25,784
- Rhonda and Matt
what’s-his-face--
- [Susan, Bret] Kellner.

156
00:11:25,785 --> 00:11:28,689
They’re missing. They haven’t
been declared dead.

157
00:11:29,289 --> 00:11:30,624
Maybe they ran away.

158
00:11:32,192 --> 00:11:34,161
Oh, Bret, before I forget,

159
00:11:34,394 --> 00:11:37,464
do you want to go shopping
with me after school
for homecoming?

160
00:11:37,764 --> 00:11:39,866
- Tuxedo Warehouse?
- [Robert] Tuxedo Warehouse?

161
00:11:40,700 --> 00:11:42,970
- Is it too, uh...
- [Robert] <i>You guys have
that on the coast?</i>

162
00:11:43,136 --> 00:11:44,604
...I don’t know, <i>GQ</i>?

163
00:11:46,239 --> 00:11:49,142
I think maybe not <i>GQ</i> enough.

164
00:11:49,342 --> 00:11:50,543
[Susan groans]

165
00:11:50,544 --> 00:11:52,879
Homecoming.
I don’t even want to go.

166
00:11:53,046 --> 00:11:56,916
What do you mean you
don’t wanna go? W-Why do you
keep saying that repeatedly?

167
00:11:56,917 --> 00:11:58,752
You have to go.

168
00:11:58,753 --> 00:12:01,021
You and Thom
are totally gonna win
homecoming king and queen.

169
00:12:01,121 --> 00:12:03,290
Aren’t we a little old
for homecoming?

170
00:12:03,423 --> 00:12:04,858
[Robert] I would agree
with that.

171
00:12:05,125 --> 00:12:07,726
Homecoming feels like a word
that’s, you know,

172
00:12:07,727 --> 00:12:10,696
partial to overweight
Midwesterners

173
00:12:10,697 --> 00:12:15,301
who make floats out of
toilet paper scraps
and suffer from gout.

174
00:12:15,302 --> 00:12:17,170
[Debbie, Susan chuckle]

175
00:12:18,405 --> 00:12:20,941
It’s not like I even have
an outfit.

176
00:12:21,408 --> 00:12:23,309
You know what you would
look great in?

177
00:12:23,310 --> 00:12:27,381
A simple black slip dress,
something fresh, modern.

178
00:12:27,514 --> 00:12:29,616
- [Susan] Hmm.
- [Robert] No jewelry.

179
00:12:29,617 --> 00:12:31,318
Just décolletage.

180
00:12:33,220 --> 00:12:34,455
Décolletage?

181
00:12:34,554 --> 00:12:36,690
Maybe we should cancel
the party too, you know.

182
00:12:36,790 --> 00:12:39,026
It’s Robert’s
big welcome party, Bret.

183
00:12:39,159 --> 00:12:40,794
It’s my big
welcome party, Bret.

184
00:12:41,027 --> 00:12:43,496
It’s gonna be like
Capote’s Black and White Ball.

185
00:12:43,497 --> 00:12:46,466
I’m so excited, thrilled.

186
00:12:47,034 --> 00:12:48,401
Et cetera.

187
00:12:54,040 --> 00:12:55,108
Bret’s right.

188
00:12:55,308 --> 00:12:56,710
We should do something.

189
00:12:57,911 --> 00:12:59,445
Maybe we should post flyers.

190
00:12:59,446 --> 00:13:02,216
That’s a great idea.
Do something proactive.

191
00:13:02,716 --> 00:13:05,151
Why don’t me and you
do it together, Susan?

192
00:13:08,722 --> 00:13:10,624
[♪ distorted eerie music plays]

193
00:13:12,292 --> 00:13:14,294
Waitress,
some more fries, please.

194
00:13:16,296 --> 00:13:21,202
We’re asking anyone
with information about Rhonda
to please come forward.

195
00:13:21,669 --> 00:13:23,070
You won’t be in trouble.

196
00:13:23,670 --> 00:13:27,707
The only way we’re gonna
find these kids
is if we look.

197
00:13:28,809 --> 00:13:30,443
Or if they show up.

198
00:13:31,678 --> 00:13:33,013
Right.

199
00:13:35,248 --> 00:13:39,086
All of us have been haunted
by the story of Etan Patz,

200
00:13:39,286 --> 00:13:43,490
six-year-old boy
who disappeared outside
his home in SoHo, New York.

201
00:13:43,924 --> 00:13:45,658
Was he kidnapped?

202
00:13:45,659 --> 00:13:46,927
Was he murdered?

203
00:13:47,227 --> 00:13:48,595
We don’t know.

204
00:13:49,630 --> 00:13:51,330
What we do know

205
00:13:51,331 --> 00:13:54,233
is that because his picture
appeared on every milk
carton in America,

206
00:13:54,234 --> 00:13:59,172
his face is burned into
the minds of the people who
are going to bring him home.

207
00:14:01,274 --> 00:14:05,479
That is why we are
handing out bottles
of Really Rhonda water

208
00:14:06,146 --> 00:14:07,980
to increase awareness--

209
00:14:07,981 --> 00:14:09,650
- And hydration.
- [chuckles softly]

210
00:14:10,351 --> 00:14:15,188
If you can’t find a Perrier,
reach for a Really Rhonda
bottled water.

211
00:14:16,590 --> 00:14:19,759
Thanks again to Rhonda’s dad
and all the heroes at ICM

212
00:14:19,760 --> 00:14:25,332
for sponsoring our flyer,
hydration,
and the Rescue Rhonda Fund.

213
00:14:26,199 --> 00:14:27,400
Bravo, ICM.

214
00:14:35,308 --> 00:14:37,711
Now, AV club, hit it.

215
00:14:38,612 --> 00:14:41,080
[♪ "Help Me, Rhonda" playing]

216
00:14:41,081 --> 00:14:42,983
Zero mention of Matt.

217
00:14:43,416 --> 00:14:45,886
- Unbelievable.
- I’m sure they’ll get to him.

218
00:14:46,253 --> 00:14:50,656
I think the line
of awareness products for Matt
Kellner could be historic.

219
00:14:50,657 --> 00:14:50,689
I think the line
of awareness products for Matt
Kellner could be historic.
"Bring Matt Home" pencils.

